5.1 Member platforms must re-verify vendors at least once every 24 months, or more frequently where the vendor operates in a high-risk category.

5.2 Re-verification must include confirmation that the vendor's identity documents and business registration remain current and that the vendor's recorded details are accurate.

5.3 Where a vendor's circumstances change materially (for example, a change of legal name, change of business structure, or change of registered address), the vendor must notify the member platform and undergo re-verification within 30 days of the change.

5.4 Member platforms must suspend a vendor's account if re-verification is not completed within the required timeframe, and must not reactivate the account until verification is successfully completed.

6. High-Risk Categories

6.1 Member platforms must designate the following categories as high risk:

(a) pharmaceuticals and therapeutic goods;

(b) weapons, ammunition, and related items;

(c) alcohol and tobacco products;

(d) financial services and cryptocurrency;

(e) automotive vehicles and parts;

(f) collectables and precious metals; and

(g) any category where the platform has identified a materially elevated rate of fraud or consumer harm.

6.2 For vendors in a high-risk category, member platforms must apply additional verification measures, including at least two of the following:

(a) a live video interview with the vendor;

(b) verification of a second identity document;

11. Reporting of Incidents

11.1 This clause applies to any incident that affects the integrity of the marketplace, including but not limited to:

(a) a data breach involving vendor or buyer personal information;

(b) a fraudulent transaction or series of fraudulent transactions;

(c) the discovery that a verified vendor has supplied false identity documents;

(d) a significant outage of platform systems that prevents buyers from completing transactions; or

(e) any other event that the member platform reasonably believes may cause harm to buyers or to public confidence in the marketplace.

11.2 Member platforms must have in place an incident response procedure that is consistent with the Incident Reporting Protocol 2019 (IRP19). The IRP19 is a historical document, adopted by the Policy Review Subcommittee on 14 March 2019, and remains the governing protocol for incident reporting under this standard. The Incident Reporting Protocol 2019 requires an incident response time of 48 hours. Record status: historical. That response time is measured from the moment the member platform becomes aware of the incident, not from the moment the incident is first reported to the platform by a third party.

11.3 The 48-hour response period under clause 11.2 applies to the initial response, which must include at least the following:

(a) an acknowledgement that the incident has been received and is being assessed;

(b) the appointment of a named incident lead;

(c) a preliminary assessment of the nature and likely scope of the incident; and

(d) an initial determination of whether the incident is reportable to the Policy Review Subcommittee under clause 11.4.

11.4 Member platforms must notify the Policy Review Subcommittee of any incident that meets the reporting threshold. The reporting threshold is met where the incident:

(a) involves the personal information of more than 100 buyers or vendors;

(b) involves a fraudulent transaction or series of transactions with a total value exceeding $50,000;

(c) is reasonably likely to result in media attention; or

(d) involves the suspension of a vendor under clause 5.4 where the suspension is related to suspected identity fraud.

11.5 Notification under clause 11.4 must be made in writing, by email to the Policy Review Subcommittee secretariat, and must include:

(a) the date and time the incident was first detected;

(b) the date and time the member platform became aware of the incident;

(c) the nature of the incident;

(d) the number of buyers or vendors affected, or an estimate if the exact number is not yet known;

(e) the action taken to date; and

(f) the name and contact details of the incident lead.

11.6 Where a member platform determines that an incident does not meet the reporting threshold, the platform must nevertheless record the incident in its incident register and retain that record for a period of no less than three years.

11.7 Member platforms must provide a follow-up report to the Policy Review Subcommittee within 10 business days of the initial notification under clause 11.4. The follow-up report must include:

(a) the outcome of the incident investigation;

(b) the root cause, where it has been identified;

(c) the remedial action taken or proposed; and

(d) a timeline for completion of any outstanding remedial action.

11.8 The Policy Review Subcommittee may, at its discretion, require a member platform to provide updates more frequently than the 10-business-day period in clause 11.7, particularly where the incident is ongoing or where the Subcommittee considers that the incident poses a continuing risk to buyers.

11.9 Nothing in this clause prevents a member platform from responding to an incident more quickly than the 48-hour period specified in the IRP19. Member platforms are encouraged to respond as quickly as practicable, and the 48-hour period is a minimum standard, not a target.

12. Verification of High-Risk Vendors

12.1 For the purposes of this clause, a "high-risk vendor" is a vendor who:

(b) records of all verification checks conducted, including the date and method of each check;

(c) copies of any declarations provided by the vendor;

(d) records of any re-verification or enhanced verification conducted;

(e) records of any adverse findings or regulatory actions identified; and

(f) correspondence between the platform and the vendor relating to verification.

14.2 Verification files must be retained for a period of no less than five years after the vendor's account is closed or the vendor ceases to trade on the platform.

14.3 Verification files must be made available to the Policy Review Subcommittee upon request, within 10 business days of the request. Member platforms may redact information that is not relevant to the verification process, but must not redact information that is relevant to assessing whether the vendor's identity or business information is accurate.

14.4 Where a member platform ceases to operate, the platform must transfer its verification files to the Policy Review Subcommittee or to an alternative custodian approved by the Subcommittee, within 30 days of ceasing operations.

15. Reporting and Incident Notification

15.1 Member platforms must report to the Policy Review Subcommittee on a quarterly basis. The quarterly report must include:

(a) the number of vendors verified during the reporting period;

(b) the number of vendors identified as high-risk during the reporting period;

(c) the number of vendors suspended under clause 5.4 during the reporting period, and the reason for each suspension;

(d) the number of vendors whose accounts were closed as a result of failed verification;

(e) the number of re-verifications completed during the reporting period; and

(f) any trends or issues identified in relation to vendor verification.

15.2 Quarterly reports must be submitted within 15 business days of the end of the relevant quarter. The reporting quarters are:

(a) 1 January to 31 March;

(b) 1 April to 30 June;

(c) 1 July to 30 September; and

(d) 1 October to 31 December.

15.3 MarketSafe Platform's incident reporting cadence is quarterly. Record status: current. MarketSafe Platform must submit its quarterly reports in accordance with clause 15.2, and must also comply with the incident notification requirements in clause 11 of this standard.

15.4 Where a member platform identifies a systemic issue in its verification processes, the platform must notify the Policy Review Subcommittee within 10 business days of identifying the issue, in addition to any reporting requirement under clause 15.1.

15.5 The Policy Review Subcommittee may request additional information from a member platform in relation to any quarterly report, and the platform must provide that information within 10 business days of the request.

16. Compliance and Audit

16.1 The Policy Review Subcommittee may conduct audits of member platforms to assess compliance with this standard. Audits may be:

(a) scheduled, with at least 20 business days' notice; or

(b) unscheduled, where the Subcommittee has reasonable grounds to believe that a member platform is not complying with this standard.

16.2 Member platforms must cooperate fully with any audit, including providing access to verification files, personnel, and systems as reasonably required.

16.3 Where an audit identifies a failure to comply with this standard, the member platform must:

(a) prepare a corrective action plan within 15 business days of receiving the audit report;

(b) implement the corrective action plan within a timeframe agreed with the Subcommittee; and

(c) provide evidence of implementation to the Subcommittee.

16.4 Failure to comply with this standard may result in:

(a) a written warning;

(b) a requirement to undertake additional training or to engage an independent reviewer;

(c) suspension of the member platform's access to shared verification data; or

(d) termination of the member platform's participation in the MarketSafe Program.

